---
import Article from '../../components/Article.astro';

const title = 'Hearts vs Spades: What’s the Difference? | CardHearth';
const description =
  'Hearts vs Spades compared: partnerships, bidding, trump, scoring and how each game feels — pick the trick-taking game that fits you.';
const updated = '2026-06-14';
const heading = 'Hearts vs Spades';
const subtitle =
  'Both are four-player trick-taking classics, but they pull in opposite directions — one is about dodging, the other about delivering. Here’s how they differ and which to play.';

const breadcrumb = [
  { name: 'CardHearth', href: '/' },
  { name: 'Guides', href: '/guides/' },
  { name: 'Hearts vs Spades', href: '/guides/hearts-vs-spades/' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is Hearts or Spades harder?',
    a: 'They are hard in different ways. Spades adds bidding and partnership coordination, so there is more to plan and a partner to read. Hearts has no bidding or partners but demands careful card-counting to dodge points and judge the moon. Most players find Spades has the steeper learning curve because of bidding.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do Hearts and Spades use trump?',
    a: 'Spades does — the spade suit is always trump. Hearts has no trump suit at all; the highest card of the led suit simply wins each trick. That single difference changes how both games are played.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which is better for playing solo against the computer?',
    a: 'Hearts is every-player-for-themselves, so playing solo against three AI opponents feels completely natural. Spades is a partnership game, so solo play means trusting a computer partner — fun, but a different experience. Both are free to play on CardHearth.',
  },
];

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'Hearts vs Spades: What’s the Difference?',
    description,
    datePublished: updated,
    dateModified: updated,
    author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Jared', url: 'https://cardhearth.com/about/' },
    publisher: {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: 'CardHearth',
      url: 'https://cardhearth.com/',
      logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://cardhearth.com/logo.png' },
    },
    mainEntityOfPage: 'https://cardhearth.com/guides/hearts-vs-spades/',
    image: 'https://cardhearth.com/og/hearts.png',
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: breadcrumb.map((c, i) => ({
      '@type': 'ListItem',
      position: i + 1,
      name: c.name,
      item: `https://cardhearth.com${c.href}`,
    })),
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: f.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
    })),
  },
];
---

<Article
  title={title}
  description={description}
  heading={heading}
  subtitle={subtitle}
  updated={updated}
  schema={schema}
  breadcrumb={breadcrumb}
  cta={{ href: '/hearts/', label: 'Play Hearts' }}
>
  <p>
    Hearts and Spades are the two most popular trick-taking card games, and they share a lot of DNA:
    four players, a standard 52-card deck, 13 tricks per hand, and the rule that you must follow the
    led suit. But the goals are opposites. In <a href="/hearts/">Hearts</a> you're trying to
    <em>avoid</em> winning the wrong cards; in <a href="/spades/">Spades</a> you commit to a number of
    tricks and have to <em>deliver</em>. Here's how they compare.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>At a glance</th><th>Hearts</th><th>Spades</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Players</th><td>4, every player for themselves</td><td>4, in two partnerships</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Trump</th><td>None</td><td>Spades, always</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Bidding</th><td>None</td><td>Bid your tricks each hand</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Goal</th><td>Avoid points — lowest wins</td><td>Make your bid — highest wins</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Signature move</th><td>Shoot the moon</td><td>Bid nil</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Game to</th><td>100 (low wins)</td><td>500</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What they share</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Four players and a full 52-card deck, aces high.</li>
    <li>Thirteen tricks per hand; you must follow the led suit if you can.</li>
    <li>The game runs over several hands to a target score.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The core difference: avoid vs deliver</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Hearts is a game of avoidance.</strong> Every heart costs a point and the Queen of Spades
    costs thirteen, so you spend the hand ducking tricks and sloughing dangerous cards onto opponents.
    The <strong>lowest</strong> score wins. <strong>Spades is a game of contracts.</strong> Before
    play, your team bids how many tricks it will take, and you're scored on hitting that number — the
    <strong>highest</strong> score wins.
  </p>

  <h2>Partnerships and bidding</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Hearts has no partners and no bidding</strong> — it's every player for themselves, and
    the only pre-play decision is the three-card pass. <strong>Spades is a partnership game with
    bidding</strong>: you and the player across the table combine your bids into one contract, and
    coordinating with your partner (especially covering a <a href="/guides/spades-rules/">nil bid</a>)
    is half the game.
  </p>

  <h2>Trump</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Spades always has trump — the spade suit.</strong> A spade beats any other suit, which
    creates the whole strategy of ruffing and drawing trumps. <strong>Hearts has no trump at all</strong>;
    the highest card of the led suit wins, full stop. If you can't follow suit in Hearts, your off-suit
    card can never win — which is exactly how you dump points.
  </p>

  <h2>Scoring and length</h2>
  <p>
    Hearts has 26 points on the table each hand (one per heart, 13 for the Queen of Spades) and runs
    until someone hits 100 — then the lowest score wins. Spades scores 10 points per bid trick, with
    bags and nil bids swinging things, and runs to 500. Spades games tend to run a little longer and
    swingier thanks to nil bids and the bag penalty.
  </p>

  <h2>The signature move</h2>
  <p>
    Each game has one spectacular play. In Hearts it's <strong>shooting the moon</strong> — take all
    26 points yourself and everyone else eats 26 instead. In Spades it's the <strong>nil bid</strong>
    — promise zero tricks for a 100-point swing. Both reward nerve and reading the table.
  </p>

  <h2>Which should you play?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Play Hearts</strong> if you want a solo, every-player-for-themselves game with tense
      card-counting and no partner to depend on.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Play Spades</strong> if you enjoy bidding, planning, and teamwork — predicting your
      tricks and pulling them off with a partner.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>Both are free, with no sign-up — so the honest answer is to try both and see which grabs you.</p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  {
    faq.map((f) => (
      <details class="faq">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold">{f.q}</summary>
        <p class="mt-2 opacity-90">{f.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))
  }

  <h2>Keep going</h2>
  <p>
    Try them both: <a href="/hearts/">play Hearts</a> or <a href="/spades/">play Spades</a>. Learn the
    details in the <a href="/guides/hearts-rules/">Hearts rules</a> and
    <a href="/guides/spades-rules/">Spades rules</a>, see all four card games in our
    <a href="/guides/best-free-card-games/">best free card games</a> roundup, or browse the
    <a href="/guides/">guides hub</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
